<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samaleswari</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Samalei</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samaleswari, or Samalei, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> goddess, the presiding deity of Sambalpur in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Her temple on the bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahanadi">Mahanadi</a> was built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhan</a> rulers of Sambalpur, traditionally in the 16th century, and she is worshipped as a form of the mother goddess across western Odisha and neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samaleswari.jpg" alt="Samaleswari" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AmitabhPatra · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samlei_Gudi_(Samaleswari_Temple).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
